INT. JUNKYARD OFFICE TRAILER - NIGHT

A cramped trailer office. A fluorescent tube buzzes over a metal desk piled with receipts. A space heater glows orange. On the wall, a calendar from 2009. An old CB radio crackles on a shelf.

TESSA MORRIS, 16, in a hoodie too big for her, sits on a broken office chair, knees up, a Polaroid camera in her lap. Across from her, perched on the desk, is her older cousin DEKE MORRIS, 19, hoodie of his own, a rolled cigarette behind his ear.

DEKE
You don't have to do this.

TESSA
Yes I do.

DEKE
Mom's not gonna know.

TESSA
That's not why.

DEKE
Then why?

TESSA
Because he said I couldn't.

DEKE
Who said?

TESSA
Dad.

DEKE
Uncle Roy said a lot of things.

TESSA
He said I couldn't take a picture worth keeping. He said I'd quit.

DEKE
You've been at it two years.

TESSA
That's quitting to him.

She winds the camera. Deke picks at the cuff of his hoodie.

Outside, headlights sweep across the window. An engine growls, idles, and cuts out. Both of them freeze. A truck door slams. Boots crunch on gravel.

DEKE
(whispering)
It's not him.

TESSA
It could be.

DEKE
Roy doesn't drive a rig.

TESSA
He borrows one.

Deke slides off the desk and eases the trailer door open a crack. He peers out, then slips through it and pulls it shut behind him. Tessa sits alone, camera clutched in both hands, listening to the buzz of the tube.

DEKE (O.S.)
(muffled, calling out)
Hey! Gate's closed! Come back tomorrow!

A heavy voice answers, too low to understand. Deke laughs, a nervous bark.

DEKE (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Nah, man, he doesn't work here anymore!

Tessa's knuckles whiten on the camera. A long silence. The engine starts again, backs, and fades up the road.

The door opens. Deke leans in, grinning with relief, and Tessa raises the camera and the flash pops white.

DEKE
Hey!

TESSA
(smiling at last)
Keeper.

He wipes the spots from his eyes. She waves the Polaroid until the picture begins to bloom.

DEKE
Let me see.

TESSA
Not yet.

They both lean over it, shoulders touching, as his face slowly appears out of the gray.

DEKE
(simultaneously)
That's terrible.

^TESSA
(simultaneously)
That's perfect.
